      "hints": [
        "Let P be the general point on the line from part (a), then form AP=OP−OA.",
        "Because AP is perpendicular to the line direction, set AP·(1,−4,5)=0.",
        "Solve for the line parameter and substitute it back into OP, not AP, to obtain the requested position vector."
      ],
      "walkthrough": [
        "Express the position vector of a general point $P$ on line $l$ in terms of $\\lambda$:\n$$\\vec{OP} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + \\lambda \\\\ 3 - 4\\lambda \\\\ -2 + 5\\lambda \\end{pmatrix}$$",
        "Find the displacement vector $\\vec{AP} = \\vec{OP} - \\vec{OA}$ with $\\vec{OA} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$:\n$$\\vec{AP} = \\begin{pmatrix} (1 + \\lambda) - 1 \\\\ (3 - 4\\lambda) - 2 \\\\ (-2 + 5\\lambda) - 0 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\lambda \\\\ 1 - 4\\lambda \\\\ -2 + 5\\lambda \\end{pmatrix}$$",
        "Set the scalar product of $\\vec{AP}$ with the direction vector $\\mathbf{d} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 5 \\end{pmatrix}$ to zero:\n$$\\vec{AP} \\cdot \\mathbf{d} = \\lambda(1) + (1 - 4\\lambda)(-4) + (-2 + 5\\lambda)(5) = 0$$\n$$\\lambda - 4 + 16\\lambda - 10 + 25\\lambda = 0$$\n$$42\\lambda - 14 = 0 \\implies 42\\lambda = 14 \\implies \\lambda = \\dfrac{14}{42} = \\dfrac{1}{3}$$",
        "Substitute $\\lambda = \\dfrac{1}{3}$ back into $\\vec{OP}$:\n$$\\vec{OP} = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 3 - 4\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\ -2 + 5\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3} \\end{pmatrix} = \\dfrac{4}{3}\\mathbf{i} + \\dfrac{5}{3}\\mathbf{j} - \\dfrac{1}{3}\\mathbf{k}$$"
      ],
